  "id": "smith-morra-gambit",
  "name": "Smith-Morra Gambit",
  "side": "white",
  "subtitle": "Open the centre. Bring everyone.",
  "character": "Fast development / Open-file pressure",
  "ideas": {
    "idea": "Offer a second pawn so your queen-side knight can develop by recapturing. White gives up a central pawn but gets a lead in development and open lines. Your job is to make those lines matter before Black builds a compact defensive position.",
    "structure": "The c- and d-files are potential routes into Black's position. Black often keeps a d6/e6 shell: sturdy, but sometimes cramped until the pieces find squares. Pawn breaks can change everything, so watch Black's freeing central advances rather than only the extra pawn.",
    "pieces": "Develop both knights, put a bishop on an active diagonal, and castle. Rooks often belong on the central open files; the queen can support them without becoming the first attacker. Coordinate the whole army before spending more material.",
    "motifs": "Pins on a central file, pressure against d6, and a knight arriving with tempo are recurring opportunities. Black also has counterplay: queen attacks on the queen-side and tactical jumps by a knight can punish loose pieces. Check your queen's safety before pursuing a tempting pawn.",
    "declined": "A knight-development decline can lead to positions resembling a c3 Sicilian, while a pawn advance asks you to use your space sensibly. In either case you may have equal material rather than a gambit attack. Take the useful centre and develop; do not sacrifice again just to recreate the accepted line.",
    "caution": "Exchanging active pieces indiscriminately can leave you simply a pawn down. Still, refusing every trade is not a plan: evaluate which pieces actually sustain your pressure."
  },
